  "id": "overfl",
  "level": "2",
  "url": "topic-1-5-casting.html#overfl",
  "type": "Activity",
  "number": "1.5.3",
  "title": "",
  "body": "  Try the code below to see that the values given to two variables are out of range for ints. An int cannot hold that many digits! Fix the problem by deleting the last 0 in the numbers to store less digits. The last print statement shows a runtime overflow error where Integer.MAX_VALUE + 1 wraps around to evaluate to Integer.MIN_VALUE which is an incorrect result for the expression.    public class TestOverflow { public static void main(String[] args) { int id = 2147483650; \/\/ too large int negative = -2147483650; \/\/ out of range System.out.println(id); System.out.println(negative); System.out.println(Integer.MAX_VALUE); System.out.println((Integer.MAX_VALUE + 1) + \" overflow will print an incorrect result!\"); } }  import static org.junit.Assert.*; import org.junit.*; import java.io.*; public class RunestoneTests extends CodeTestHelper { @Test public void testMain() throws IOException { String output = getMethodOutput(\"main\"); String expect = \"214748365\\n-214748365\\n\"; boolean passed = getResults( expect, output, \"Fixed out of range errors\"); assertTrue(passed); } }   "
