  "title": "",
  "body": "  Given the following code which of the answers best describes the conditions needed for temp to be true when it is returned?   boolean temp = false; int count = 0; for ( int testVal : a) { if ( testVal == val ) count++; } temp = count > 1; return temp;       Whenever the first element in a is equal to val    What is count for?      Whenever a contains any element which equals val.    It only sets temp to true when count is greater than 1.      Whenever more than 1 element in a is equal to val.    This increments count once for each value in a that is equal to val. It returns true when count is greater than 1.      Whenever exactly 1 element in a is equal to val.    This would be true if it was temp = count == 1.      Whenever the last element in a is equal to val.    This could be one line of code return (a[a.length-1] == val).     "
